WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430
(soft music)

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.000
- [Wes] Conservation of
birds is a unique challenge.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.700
How do you study an
animal that spends most

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.555
of it's time hundreds of feet in the air?

00:00:08.555 --> 00:00:09.720
(bird shriek)

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.050
Sometimes, you just need to catch one.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:13.487
- Got em.

00:00:13.487 --> 00:00:17.100
(soft guitar music)

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.687
- Man, just lost in his eyes right now.

00:00:19.687 --> 00:00:22.140
(upbeat music)

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.318
- I'm Wes Larson, a wildlife biologist

00:00:24.318 --> 00:00:27.900
and lover of the outdoors for
as long as I can remember.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.180
I'm traveling the country
to find the next generation

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.520
of conservationists.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.180
The people on the front lines of the fight

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.450
to understand our planet
and protect the animals

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:36.430
we share it with.

00:00:41.522 --> 00:00:43.740
(upbeat music)

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.560
Today, I'm headed into the desert,

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.620
due West of Salt Lake City.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:50.160
I'm on my way to a remote research station

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.910
in the Goshute Mountains of Nevada.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.960
You can just barely
start to see the Goshutes

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.180
out there in the distance.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.130
So remote, in fact, that
researchers spend months

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:00.830
at a time on site.

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:03.160
You have to be an especially
hardcore conservationist

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.403
to sign up for this job.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.351
- Hey, man.

00:01:08.351 --> 00:01:09.184
- You must be Wes.

00:01:09.184 --> 00:01:10.100
- You must be Jesse.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.071
- Ya, man. Nice to meet you.

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:11.904
- Good to meet you, too.

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:12.737
- You ready to see some hawks?

00:01:12.737 --> 00:01:13.590
- I am.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.710
- Jesse Watson is a research
biologist with Hawkwatch.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.700
This is his second season on the mountain,

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.780
but he's been working with
birds since he was a kid.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.130
- This is a long-term
raptor monitoring site.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.150
We've been counting
and catching hawks here

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.630
since the early 80s, so about 35 years.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.380
- Were gonna hike all
the way up to 9,000 feet?

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.790
- [Jesse] Ya, man. All the way to the top.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:35.600
- There are no roads to the camp.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.770
Supplies are flown in by helicopter,

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.763
carried up on your back.

00:01:39.763 --> 00:01:41.430
- It starts getting fairly,

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:42.850
fairly steep in this next section,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.100
a lot of switchbacks.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.120
- [Wes] But it's worth it for these birds.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.310
- [Jesse] Raptors are kind of
a barometer for the ecosystem

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:50.760
they're at the top of the food chain,

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.380
and so, if they're not doing well,

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.020
then there's an indication
that something is wrong.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.980
- [Wes] The Hawkwalk
researchers live full time

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.040
on the mountain,

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.253
counting hawks by day and
sleeping in tents at night.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:03.910
- Welcome to camp.

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:05.900
- And, uh, where's the hot tub?

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:06.756
- Ya.

00:02:06.756 --> 00:02:09.500
(birds chirping)

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.880
- [Wes] For Halkwatch to
study these birds up close,

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.372
they have to catch them first.

00:02:13.372 --> 00:02:14.500
(soft guitar music)

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.040
And here is where an unexpected member

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.860
of the team comes into play.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.390
- So raptors are
migrating along the ridge;

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:20.600
they're hungry,

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.540
so we use pigeons as part of our team

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:24.700
to help us capture them.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.500
- And I noticed you've got
these harnesses for your pigeons

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:28.800
just to make sure that, you know, if

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.020
the hawk comes in it's not
actually gonna hurt it.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.390
- Absolutely, I mean
they're pretty critical

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.090
member of our team.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.230
And we definitely treat them
with the utmost respect.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.313
(soft guitar music)

00:02:40.313 --> 00:02:42.080
- With our pigeons safely in place,

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.870
we settle into the trapping
blind and meet Scott,

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.240
one of Hawkwatch's veteran crew leaders.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:48.980
- So how we doing Scott?

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:49.830
How's the flight?

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:50.860
- Good, really good.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.440
It's actually pushing our biggest day,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.910
there a lot of birds
throwing up out there.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.360
- [Wes] And before we knew it

00:02:57.809 --> 00:02:58.722
- Got 'em.

00:02:58.722 --> 00:03:02.222
(upbeat thundering music)

00:03:07.374 --> 00:03:09.804
- You're just wondering,
what's going on right now, huh.

00:03:09.804 --> 00:03:11.260
(chirp)

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:12.880
- So ya, we've been in this blind like,

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.210
I don't know five minutes

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:16.090
we already caught a Coopers hawk.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:18.890
- Catching the birds doesn't
hurt them in any way.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:22.360
OK, sweetie, there you
go, right in the can.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.540
I see you're being very
delicate with those talons

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:25.373
as you pull it out.

00:03:25.373 --> 00:03:27.290
- Ya, so the thing you really
gotta watch with raptors

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.580
is their feet and their
bill are the two things

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.790
that can really tear you up.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.003
- In just a couple of minutes,

00:03:33.003 --> 00:03:36.254
each bird is given a
checkup and a tracking band.

00:03:36.254 --> 00:03:37.234
She's banded.

00:03:37.234 --> 00:03:38.130
- She's banded.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:39.470
- Ya, got jewelry.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.800
- It's so crazy to me that how calm she is

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.420
as she's in this little tube.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:44.253
- Right.

00:03:44.253 --> 00:03:45.086
- Like she's not chirping,

00:03:45.086 --> 00:03:45.919
she's not ...
- Yep.

00:03:45.919 --> 00:03:48.440
- struggling, she's just totally relaxed.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:49.940
- So those are the main body measurements,

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:51.860
weight, wing chord and standard tail.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:52.730
- Ya.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.010
The Goshute Mountains are
a kind of super highway

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:56.570
for migrating raptors.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.100
In over just a couple of hours,

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.320
we banded seven different birds.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:01.270
Overall at this site,

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.570
Hawkwatch researchers
have assessed and banded

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:06.940
over 65,000 different raptors.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.440
- Ya, so the next step is
basically releasing her.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.877
- So this one's ready
to go, we banded her.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:15.903
She is definitely ready.

00:04:20.121 --> 00:04:22.538
(calm music)

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.950
Just the way they like, look at you

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:28.360
it's just so intent.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.251
Like, it's like they're looking
straight through you almost.

00:04:30.251 --> 00:04:31.669
- Uh, huh.

00:04:31.669 --> 00:04:32.752
- Ready high?

00:04:36.633 --> 00:04:38.050
- There she goes.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.340
- Pretty cool. I bet you
don't get tired of that.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.435
- No, never gets old.

00:04:42.435 --> 00:04:43.268
- Ya.

00:04:43.268 --> 00:04:44.373
- Definitely not. And
there she is right there.

00:04:44.373 --> 00:04:45.600
- Ya.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.120
While catching and banding is crucial

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.170
for assessing individual birds,

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:50.900
the status of raptor population's

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.860
is what Jesse cares about most.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.603
So the bulk of the team's
work looks like this.

00:04:59.814 --> 00:05:01.970
(upbeat music)

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.540
During migration season in the fall,

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.050
the researchers are out
here every single day.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.690
Literally counting each
bird that flies by.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:11.580
Through the years, they've
counted over 13 million,

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:12.713
one by one.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.230
- You want to kinda start below horizon

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:17.620
and just kinda scan up slowly.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:19.970
- And, if I see a bird,
I just yell it out?

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:20.833
- Ya.
- Cool.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.150
Counting birds can be tedious.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:28.773
It means watching the skies for hours.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:31.670
So what's that one down there?

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:32.503
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:05:32.503 --> 00:05:33.687
- Right over the ridge.

00:05:33.687 --> 00:05:35.270
- Oh ya, that's an adult Red-tail.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:37.380
So, Wes got a bird down here

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:39.060
just going down low.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:40.130
The counting itself is

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:41.535
the most important thing that we do.

00:05:41.535 --> 00:05:43.234
Year after year after year
we can get those trends

00:05:43.234 --> 00:05:45.470
so we can see like, hey,

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:47.380
these numbers are going
up, or they're going down.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:48.710
We've seen declines in species

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:50.520
like Golden eagles and American kestrel's

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:52.620
on migration through these accounts.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:53.453
- The idea that I'm getting

00:05:53.453 --> 00:05:56.907
is that if you guys like
weren't up here on this ridge

00:05:56.907 --> 00:05:59.020
in the cold, in the wind

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:02.880
for months at a time, all day
long counting these birds,

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.320
like, no one would really have any idea

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:07.600
what the numbers are doing.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.500
- Absolutely, yeah, it's
a real dedicated effort.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.980
You really have to commend
our crews that are counting

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:13.336
and doing the work.

00:06:13.336 --> 00:06:16.480
(soft music)

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:17.800
- When you work in conservation,

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.270
you sometimes forget that
when people see the studies,

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:21.450
they don't see the hard work,

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:23.090
meticulous data collection

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.223
and luck that you need to get the results.

00:06:25.223 --> 00:06:26.580
(soft music)

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:27.710
Standing on this mountain,

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.770
seeing Hawkwatch's efforts first-hand

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:32.080
is a great reminder of
the real tangible work

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.855
that goes into this kind of conservation.

00:06:34.855 --> 00:06:37.938
(soft flowing music)

00:06:43.968 --> 00:06:45.885
(ding)

